WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.729
Mixture models usually blend simple distributions
by adding them.

00:00:04.809 --> 00:00:08.640
But what if a mixture could also subtract
probability mass?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.164
Published at ICLR 2024,

00:00:10.244 --> 00:00:15.004
this paper shows how to learn deep subtractive
mixtures by squaring them.

00:00:15.084 --> 00:00:20.373
Squaring keeps the model a valid distribution while
allowing negative parameters,

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:26.472
and the authors prove these squared non-monotonic
circuits can be exponentially more compact

00:00:26.552 --> 00:00:28.593
than ordinary additive mixtures,

00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:34.360
then confirm the gain on real-world density
estimation and language-model distillation.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.662
Traditional mixture models build complex
distributions by blending simple ones additively.

00:00:40.742 --> 00:00:43.663
That works, but it can be wildly inefficient.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:47.597
If the target distribution has gaps or holes in its
domain,

00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:52.932
an additive mixture must stack up many components
just to carve those holes out.

00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:59.201
The natural fix is to let some components subtract
probability mass instead of only adding it.

00:00:59.281 --> 00:01:01.735
The catch: once you allow subtraction,

00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:06.069
the model can dip below zero and stop being a valid
distribution,

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:08.670
and learning it becomes genuinely hard.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.578
So why revisit this now?

00:01:10.658 --> 00:01:18.017
Because subtraction pays off: a mixture that can
cancel mass captures complex shapes with far fewer components.

00:01:18.097 --> 00:01:21.904
The problem is guaranteeing the result stays non-negative.

00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:25.992
Energy-based models enforce non-negativity by
exponentiation,

00:01:26.072 --> 00:01:28.539
but then lose tractable normalization.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:31.152
Meanwhile several separate communities,

00:01:31.232 --> 00:01:35.307
from signal processing to kernel methods to quantum
mechanics,

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:40.869
each rediscovered a squaring trick to enforce
non-negativity, but without a common,

00:01:40.949 --> 00:01:43.550
tractable framework tying them together.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.642
This work makes four contributions.

00:01:47.722 --> 00:01:51.434
First, a general framework for representing
subtractive mixtures by squaring,

00:01:51.514 --> 00:01:55.225
expressed in the language of tensorized
probabilistic circuits.

00:01:55.305 --> 00:01:59.017
Second,

00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:02.809
it shows these squared non-monotonic circuits
generalize several apparently different models with negative parameters,

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:06.601
including square-root-of-density models, positive
semi-definite kernel models,

00:02:06.681 --> 00:02:10.393
and Born machines from quantum physics.

00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:14.185
Third,

00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:17.976
it proves an exponential lower bound: functions a
single squared circuit encodes compactly

00:02:18.056 --> 00:02:21.768
need exponentially more units in any monotonic circuit.

00:02:21.848 --> 00:02:25.560
And fourth, it backs this with experiments on
real-world data.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.895
Here is the core idea.

00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:33.929
Take a mixture with unconstrained real weights,

00:02:34.009 --> 00:02:37.964
so components can have negative coefficients, and
simply square the whole thing.

00:02:38.044 --> 00:02:41.998
Squaring forces the output to be non-negative no
matter what the weights are.

00:02:42.078 --> 00:02:46.033
Expanding the square turns a K-component mixture
into a sum over all pairs of components,

00:02:46.113 --> 00:02:50.067
a product-of-experts form whose partition function
can still be computed in closed form for many families.

00:02:50.147 --> 00:02:54.102
To go deep,

00:02:54.182 --> 00:02:58.136
the authors square a tensorized
structured-decomposable circuit layer by layer.

00:02:58.216 --> 00:03:02.171
Each squared layer holds a quadratic number of
units but still outputs a vector,

00:03:02.251 --> 00:03:06.205
so the whole model trains efficiently with gradient
descent,

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:10.240
computing the normalizer just once per batch.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.365
The experiments cover three regimes.

00:03:13.445 --> 00:03:19.092
First, two-dimensional synthetic densities, both
continuous and discrete,

00:03:19.172 --> 00:03:25.839
where you can literally see how well each model
captures rings and other holey shapes.

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:32.116
Second, five standard UCI datasets: Power, Gas,
Hepmass, MiniBooNE, and BSDS300.

00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:37.530
And third, distilling sentences sampled from the
GPT2 language model.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:43.728
Baselines span full-covariance Gaussians,
normalizing flows like RealNVP, MADE,

00:03:43.808 --> 00:03:50.240
MAF and NSF, monotonic probabilistic circuits, and
tensor-train density estimators.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.294
The headline finding is consistent: at the same
model size,

00:03:54.374 --> 00:04:00.021
squared non-monotonic circuits reach higher
log-likelihoods than monotonic circuits across

00:04:00.101 --> 00:04:01.867
the density-estimation tasks.

00:04:01.947 --> 00:04:06.448
On the two-dimensional ring distributions, plain
squaring already helps,

00:04:06.528 --> 00:04:12.621
but it is the negative parameters that let the
model actually carve out the holes in the density.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:14.784
And on the GPT2 distillation task,

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:19.493
the squared circuits scale better and get closer to
GPT2's own likelihood,

00:04:19.573 --> 00:04:24.520
approximating the intractable model more faithfully
than monotonic circuits do.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.064
To confirm the gains come from subtraction and not
just squaring,

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:32.655
the authors compare against squared monotonic circuits,

00:04:32.735 --> 00:04:35.920
which square but keep all parameters non-negative.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.858
The squared non-monotonic circuits still win,

00:04:38.938 --> 00:04:42.188
so the negative parameters are doing the real work.

00:04:42.268 --> 00:04:47.738
Two other patterns emerge: binary-tree region
graphs generally beat linear-tree ones,

00:04:47.818 --> 00:04:49.566
and the input layer matters,

00:04:49.646 --> 00:04:54.594
with splines helping most on continuous data and
embeddings on discrete data.

00:04:54.674 --> 00:04:58.250
On some discrete image-mass tasks the advantage narrows.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.621
A few numbers make the point.

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:06.692
Squaring a mixture with K components encodes on the
order of K-squared-over-two pairwise

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:10.763
components while reusing the same K parameters,

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:14.834
which is where the compactness comes from,

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:18.905
and the paper proves this gap over monotonic
circuits is exponential.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:22.975
Empirically,

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:27.046
the squared non-monotonic circuits post the best
test log-likelihoods among tractable models on several UCI datasets,

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:31.117
for example zero point six two on Power, about
eleven on Gas,

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:35.188
minus twenty point four on Hepmass, and minus
twenty-six point seven on MiniBooNE.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.259
On the language data they climb toward GPT2's own
likelihood of around minus fifty-two,

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:43.330
while monotonic circuits flatten out.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.118
The takeaway is simple.

00:05:45.198 --> 00:05:48.159
Squaring turns subtractive mixtures into valid,

00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:53.657
tractable probabilistic models that need
exponentially fewer components than additive

00:05:53.737 --> 00:05:56.051
mixtures for the same expressiveness.

00:05:56.131 --> 00:06:00.709
Along the way, this one framework unifies
square-root-of-density models,

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:03.103
positive semi-definite kernel models,

00:06:03.183 --> 00:06:07.890
and quantum Born machines under a single
property-driven view of circuits,

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:09.896
making subtraction a practical,

00:06:09.976 --> 00:06:14.230
first-class tool for compact and expressive
probabilistic modeling.
